From 5617fd59879995b3cbe996e3f0f9bade563c7128 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <stew675@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 05:29:21 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 01/15] rdna-boosts: block 01: adaptive MTP draft depth

Adaptive MTP draft depth (--spec-type draft-mtp-adaptive): a per-seq
credit-bucket controller tracks the acceptance of MTP-drafted tokens and
adapts the draft depth N on every verify round.  Each round moves a
bucket B by (n_accepted - depth), except that a full accept is credited
max(1, n_accepted - 1) instead, so a deeper accept is worth more and a
strong run by another speculator (which can accept far past the depth)
is credited too.  The depth climbs a step when B reaches the cap (the
drop pressure plus the climb budget) and drops a step when B falls to
zero, carrying the surplus or the deficit into the new depth's bucket.
N stays within [floor, cap]: the floor is --spec-draft-n-min-adaptive
(default 3), the cap is --spec-draft-n-max (bounded by the model MTP
layer count).  The cold start is cap - 3 bounded by the floor, because the
climb is the expensive direction and the drift settles the depth from
there; the start is only the drift's entry point, so it is overridable per
context with --spec-draft-n-start (clamped to [floor, cap]) for a workload
whose best entry point differs (a wide verify batch costs more at a deep
context); the depth resets on each new generation.

Squashed from the stew675 llama.cpp PR #27210 changeset (author of
record), refreshed 2026-09-08 to the PR-review response head
d236d41a2 (github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/pull/27210
issuecomment-5582088497).  The refresh carries the review round's
changes over the previously delivered PR state (0994374fd):

- common_params_speculative::has_mtp() helper; arg.cpp/common.cpp/
  server-context.cpp/init-result MTP-type checks refactored through it
- accept_partial() virtual + common_speculative_accept_partial() so a
  partial acceptance the context could not apply (checkpoint-restore
  path in tools/server + examples/speculative-simple) is reported once
  with the true accept count, and the following replay round (n_last
  reset) cannot feed stale draft counts to the adaptive controller; the
  non-adaptive accept path is unchanged
- adaptive depth reset moves ahead of the empty-prompt early return in
  begin(), so the depth is reset on every new generation
- --spec-draft-n-min-adaptive rejects values < 1 (invalid_argument);
  registration order/example coverage normalized; --spec-draft-n-min
  in adaptive mode warns that it is unused
- invalid adaptive range check: GGML_ABORT -> std::runtime_error
- draft-mtp + draft-mtp-adaptive together are rejected (shared ctx_dft)
- adaptive controller header comment rewritten (the constants lived in
  climb_threshold/drop_pressure tables at this point; they are now the
  credit-bucket form below)
- docs/speculative.md, tools/cli/README.md, tools/server/README.md
  document draft-mtp-adaptive + --spec-draft-n-min-adaptive
- src/models/delta-net-base.cpp: conv-state snapshot-bound rationale
  comment (only callers that roll back remove tokens this batch decoded)
- tests/test-arg-parser.cpp: stale "defaults to 2" comment fixed
  (default is 3), value-0 rejection case added

Controller behaviour was re-validated by the PR author on delta-net
hardware after the review changes (C3/C6 on Qwen3.8-27B): throughput
and acceptance match prior measurements within run-to-run noise.

Amended 2026-09-15: the mean-reverting accumulator was replaced by the
credit-bucket controller above (stew675's bucketed design) and tuned for
the delivery's acceptance, which is higher than mainline's and therefore
raises the bucket drift at every depth.  The credit function needs no
tuning: the drift's zero-crossing already lands on the throughput optimum
of every workload measured (code ~9, prose/reasoning/phase-switching at
the floor, verbatim recall at the ceiling).  The constants did:

- climb_budget grows with depth, 20 + 6*(depth - 1).  A flat 20 let six
  consecutive full accepts at depth 8 cascade the depth 9 -> 10 -> 11 ->
  12 in 16 rounds, because the credit grows with depth; a depth-scaled
  budget makes the top of the range resist a streak as the floor does.
- drop_pressure is steeper, max(60, 10*depth) instead of max(20, 4*depth),
  which damps the slow 6 <-> 12 limit cycle a flat budget produced (40
  depth changes in 477 verification rounds; each change costs a rollback
  and a re-draft).
- the cold start is cap - 3 instead of the floor (overridable per context
  with --spec-draft-n-start, clamped to [floor, cap]).  Off the floor a
  step costs ~20 net full accepts and the controller burned a third of a
  3000-token run reaching the plateau, which is the entire advantage of a
  higher ceiling.  Settling down is cheap even for a workload whose
  equilibrium is the floor, so starting near the plateau costs a reasoning
  or phase-switching workload little.

Amended 2026-09-16: the cold start was briefly moved to the midpoint of the
floor and the cap, on the theory that a lower entry point helps a short
generation at a deep context.  A same-build comparison (shallow and ~32k
context, -n 300 and -n 3000) showed the cap - 3 start is at least as fast
in every case: end-to-end throughput tracks the round count, and the
optimized multi-token verify makes a wider batch cheap, so a lower start
only lowers the settled mean depth (reasoning/prose pay nothing, recall
loses most).  The default is cap - 3 again and the start is a runtime
option (--spec-draft-n-start) rather than a constant.

Measured, Qwen3.8-27B Q8_0 x 2-card tensor split, f16 KV, -n 3000: code
ceiling-12 96.0 t/s vs ceiling-7 95.8 (was 92.8 vs 96.3, the reported
regression), 4 depth changes instead of 40; reasoning +5.0%, prose
+11.2%, code +18.8%, recall +58.7% riding at depth 12; a code <-> reasoning
phase-switching workload stays at 64.0 against its 64.3 pinned-depth
optimum.  On 1-card UD-Q4_K_XL, code ceiling-12 is 84.7 vs ceiling-7
61.4 (+37.9%).  Greedy output is unchanged: adaptive cap 7, adaptive cap
12 and fixed draft-mtp are byte-identical.

The depth state transitions are now reported at TRC (they are the
user-visible explanation of a run's decode throughput) and carry the
bucket; tests/test-speculative-adaptive.cpp is rewritten against the
bucket constants.
